# Patch 0082 — Knowledge capture: per-page notes, highlights, web clipper
# (ROADMAP Phase 7; FEATURES 6.1 + 6.2; ADR 0015). The first Phase 7 brick and
# the one the personal knowledge graph is built out of: until now Kavacha
# remembered what the user READ (patch 0078's index) and nothing about what
# they THOUGHT.
#
# THE DELETION CONTRACT IS DELIBERATELY THE INVERSE OF THE INDEX'S. ADR 0012
# made the personal index follow Places — clear history, the index goes —
# because the index is a derived cache of pages the browser happened to see.
# Nothing in this store is derived: a note is a document the user wrote and a
# clip is something they chose to keep, so history deletion must NOT touch it.
# A browser that eats your notes when you clear your history has confused
# which of the two was precious. The cost is stated rather than discovered:
# this store therefore outlives history and can surface a page the user
# believed they had erased, which is why per-item Delete, "forget this page"
# and a one-call export all exist from the first commit.
#
# THREE KINDS, ONE TABLE. `note` is at most one per URL and empty deletes it —
# clearing the box IS the delete gesture, so there is no second thing to learn.
# `highlight` is a selected passage plus an optional comment (FEATURES 6.1's
# annotation). `clip` is the readable text of the whole page at the moment it
# was saved, which is also the honest half of "offline mode": the words
# survive the site, the layout does not. A byte-for-byte archive would mean
# fetching and rewriting subresources — a different feature with a different
# threat model (ADR 0015 § Offline).
#
# URL NORMALIZATION IS PART OF THE PRODUCT, not a detail. The fragment is
# dropped so a note taken at #section-3 is the page's note when you come back
# to the top; the query string is KEPT because ?v=… is usually a different
# document. Non-http(s) URLs return "" from keyFor() and every writer refuses
# on that, which is how about:/file:/chrome: pages stay out.
#
# ONE-NOTE-PER-PAGE IS ENFORCED BY THE DATABASE (a partial UNIQUE index on
# url WHERE kind='note'), not by read-then-write in JS. Two windows on the
# same page would both find "no note" and both insert one; the upsert makes
# that impossible rather than unlikely.
#
# THE SIDEBAR IS SEPARATE FROM THE AI SIDEBAR ON PURPOSE. Folding notes into
# the surface built by patch 0080 was the cheaper option and would have made
# the browser's note-taking vanish when `kavacha.ai.enabled` went false.
# Notes need no model, so they do not get a model's master switch. Both
# register through SidebarController.registerPrefSidebar (ADR 0014's finding),
# so no upstream file is edited and a Firefox uplift cannot conflict.
#
# CHROME DOCUMENT RULES, unchanged and for the same reason as 0080: clipped
# page text and note text are UNTRUSTED strings rendered with SYSTEM
# privileges, so they reach the DOM only through textContent or
# KavachaMarkdown (which builds nodes and never parses HTML). One innerHTML
# here would turn "clip a page" into "execute that page's markup as chrome".
# Page content is read only through the KavachaIndexer actor, which already
# refuses anything that is not an http/https top frame; this patch adds one
# narrower query to it (CaptureSelection) rather than a second actor, so the
# policy is stated once and cannot drift.
#
# Icons were checked against zen-icons/jar.inc.mn before shipping: patch 0080
# found five commands pointing at SVGs that are not in the set, and a missing
# icon renders as nothing at all.
#
